  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "cmd/ocr-forgejo/main.go",
      "content": "The query mode accepts an `ocr-last-reviewed` marker from ANY PR comment that happens to contain `format.SummaryMarker`, with no check that the comment was authored by the review bot. `IssueCommentInfo` intentionally carries only ID+Body, and `ListIssueComments` returns raw Forgejo comments, so any user who can comment on the PR can post a body like `\u003c!-- ocr-summary --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- ocr-last-reviewed:\u003ccurrent head sha\u003e --\u003e` and forge the marker. Since the printed SHA is consumed by the workflow to compute the `--from` delta range for the next run (per the proposal), a forged marker can shrink or eliminate the review range; with resolution tracking it can contribute to an unearned APPROVE. Suggest gating the lookup on the bot's own comments: expose the author login in `IssueCommentInfo` (the SDK comment carries poster info) and skip comments whose author is not the bot identity returned by `WhoAmI()`.",
      "suggestion_code": "\tme, err := p.WhoAmI()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tfor i := len(comments) - 1; i \u003e= 0; i-- {\n\t\t// Only trust the bot's own summary comments: without the\n\t\t// author check any commenter can plant a forged last-reviewed\n\t\t// marker and steer the next run's delta range.\n\t\tif comments[i].Author != me || !strings.Contains(comments[i].Body, format.SummaryMarker) {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}",
      "existing_code": "\tfor i := len(comments) - 1; i \u003e= 0; i-- {\n\t\tif !strings.Contains(comments[i].Body, format.SummaryMarker) {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}",
      "start_line": 293,
      "end_line": 296,
      "category": "security",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "path": "cmd/ocr-forgejo/main.go",
      "content": "This regex literal duplicates the marker syntax that already lives in `format.LastReviewedMarkerFmt` (\"\u003c!-- ocr-last-reviewed:%s --\u003e\"). They agree today, but nothing ties them together: a future edit to the format constant without touching this regex silently yields zero matches, so the query mode degrades to always-full reviews (prints nothing, exits 0) with no error signal. Derive the pattern from the format constant so there is a single source of truth.",
      "suggestion_code": "// lastReviewedMarkerRe is derived from format.LastReviewedMarkerFmt so\n// the write-side format and the read-side pattern cannot drift.\nvar lastReviewedMarkerRe = regexp.MustCompile(\n\tstrings.Replace(regexp.QuoteMeta(format.LastReviewedMarkerFmt), \"%s\", `(\\S+)`, 1))",
      "existing_code": "var lastReviewedMarkerRe = regexp.MustCompile(`\u003c!-- ocr-last-reviewed:(\\S+) --\u003e`)",
      "start_line": 283,
      "end_line": 283,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/publish/ledger.go",
      "content": "Span staleness makes 'Valid' classification an unsound proof that \"the code under this finding was provably changed\" (as the coverage comments claim). Ledger entries carry spans recorded at a previous head's line numbering (they are only refreshed when a candidate re-reports/matches), while the coverage diff's hunk ranges are in the current head's numbering — Classify does a numeric containment check with no old→new line mapping. On a delta push that inserts/deletes lines above a tracked finding, an untouched, un-re-reported finding's stale span can numerically land inside a hunk covering different, newly inserted lines: e.g. hunk `@@ -1,5 +1,25 @@` (20 lines inserted at the top of a.go) plus an open entry `a.go L10-L20` whose real code now sits at L31-L41 → Classify(P, 10, 20) == Valid → the still-live finding is stamped ✅ RESOLVED, and with zero survivors the clean delta run then submits APPROVED via decideState (the D4 downgrade is silently bypassed) — exactly the failure mode this flag was added to prevent. The only documented edge (D3) is the safe, false-Invalid direction. Suggest anchoring resolution on congruence rather than bare containment: retain the old-side ranges per hunk in the Inventory and map the entry's stale span into the new-numbering (or union old-new span overlap across each hunk's deletion/insertion pair) before classifying; otherwise document the span-drift edge like the pure-deletion one.",
      "suggestion_code": "// resolves reports whether an unmatched open entry may resolve under this\n// coverage state at the given path and span.\n//\n// The entry span is only a proof of change when it maps into the new\n// numbering: entries are recorded at a prior head's line numbers, so the\n// coverage diff must supply the old-side ranges to bridge span drift\n// before containment is checked.\nfunc (c *Coverage) resolves(path string, start, end int64) bool {\n\tif c == nil {\n\t\treturn true // no coverage flag: full-range semantics\n\t}\n\tif c.Inv == nil {\n\t\treturn false // no coverage: resolve nothing\n\t}\n\treturn c.Inv.Classify(path, start, end) == diffinv.Valid\n}",
      "existing_code": "// resolves reports whether an unmatched open entry may resolve under this\n// coverage state at the given path and span.\nfunc (c *Coverage) resolves(path string, start, end int64) bool {\n\tif c == nil {\n\t\treturn true // no coverage flag: full-range semantics\n\t}\n\tif c.Inv == nil {\n\t\treturn false // no coverage: resolve nothing\n\t}\n\treturn c.Inv.Classify(path, start, end) == diffinv.Valid\n}",
